# Line-ending policy: the repository stores LF, and every checkout gets LF.
#
# Without this file, a clone made with the Windows default core.autocrlf=true
# checks out CRLF while git stores LF. Nothing looks wrong — until someone edits
# a file git was not already tracking as modified, at which point the *whole*
# file shows as changed because every line ending differs. `docs/hardening.md`
# did exactly that during M16: an eleven-line edit produced a 749-line diff.
#
# It also breaks a project command outright. biome.json sets "lineEnding": "lf",
# so a CRLF working tree fails `npm run format:check` on every file at once while
# `git diff` stays empty — a mismatch with no visible cause. docs/release.md §1
# records the per-clone workaround; this file removes the need for it.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary. These must never be touched: the 0x0D bytes in them are data, and
# rewriting one as a line ending corrupts the file with no error anywhere.
*.png binary
*.jar binary

# The one file that must keep CRLF: cmd.exe is the consumer, not a POSIX shell.
# Its counterpart `android/gradlew` is a shell script and takes LF from the rule
# above — it was checked in with CRLF, which is a latent failure on any Unix
# machine, since /bin/sh rejects a shebang line ending in a carriage return.
*.bat text eol=crlf
